okay today I'm going to talk to you  about percentile charts percentile  charts is type of growth chart that  monitors children's growth over time  this allows us to see if their  development is normal and healthy or  whether there needs to be some kind of  medical intervention so what we're going  to do is to see how this works so first  of all let's think what it actually  means for percentile so let's just  imagine well this is just a real kind of  rough diagram to show the different mass  of different babies so let's say there's  a million babies born in a particular  country over the course of a year some  of those babies will be very very small  so here's right are really little and of  a kind of of our light mass or low mass  some babies will kind of be average kind  of size average mass and we're going to  get some really bigger babies as well so  what we can see is we've got a huge  range of different sizes now there'd be  all of the different babies in between  as well and a kind of sliding scale if  we arrange them from the lightest to the  heaviest but I'm not going to draw all  of those in right now but just imagine  we've got a nice sliding scale to show  the smaller babies at this end and the  much bigger babies at this end so what a  percentile is is essentially what we do  is we split up all of these different  masses of babies into a hundred  different groups  that's what percent means sent whenever  you sit here and see the word sense we  think of a hundred so there's a hundred  different groups so the very very  lightest group would be percentile one  so for example if there were a million  babies in our sample size 1% of a  million is ten thousand so there'd be  ten thousand babies in this first  category there'd be ten thousand in the  second and ten thousand in this third  all the way up right in the middle the  50th percentile what that means is fifty  percent of babies are at least this mass  or bigger and fifty percent of babies of  this mass or less and right up to the  very  the very heaviest group would be that  100th percentile so we've got all of  this data now what we do with this is we  can actually track the babies kind of  development over time and this is where  the percentile chart comes in along the  x-axis we have age of the baby in months  up the y-axis what we have is the mass  of the baby in kilograms so we can see  how that changes over time so let's  first of all consider our really light  babies let's say our very first  percentile now they might start around  here and over time they're going to  increase in mass as they grow so we  should expect to see a growth like that  with our heavier babies we're going to  obviously start at a higher mass so  maybe they're and they're going to grow  over time as well now these numbers are  only approximate please don't take them  as as the real values for the first  100th percentile now a 50th one would go  somewhere in the middle so it would look  like this because what we can see is the  average kind of development of these  different groups over time so what's the  point of the growth chart we can see  this right now now if I've quickly show  you how to read this first of all let's  see at 6 months what each of these  difference percentiles way so the  average must in the sixth in the first  percentile so I just write the different  ones down here there's our first  percentile there's our 50th percentile  and there's a hundredth percentile so  the average mass of a baby in the SiC in  the first percentile after six months is  around approximately approximately 6  kilograms so all you need to do is read  up to the line and then across to the  y-axis so it's 6 months and the 50th  percentile according to this will be  around about 9 kilograms and after six  months and 100 percentile would be just  under 12 kilograms ok so that's how to  read a growth chart no it's  far as medical intervention goes doctors  will normally intervene for three  different things first of all if it's if  the baby is above the hundredth  percentile or below the first percentile  then there may well be something to look  at so if the baby  at any stage is either side of these  lines development is very very irregular  and therefore they need to look at look  into it there could be a problem with  malnutrition or  over nutrition basically needs to be  looked at secondly if there's two or  more so two plus percentile line changes  over time then we also need to look into  it something else is probably not going  quite right for example in the first  percentile if we start off here but then  have an unexpected growth spurt that  could be that could be problematic  similarly if we started off where we'd  expect to be going looking at the rest  of the percentile it shifts by two or  more percentiles the other way they  could be a problem with malnutrition  here as well the third way that we might  have an intervention of some sort is if  there's an inconsistent pattern so my  development might be going up and down  or a bit all over the place an example  of this could be from a small baby with  a very large head  so percentile charts allow us to monitor  the growth of babies over time to see if  they need help if one of these three  things happen and what a percentile is  is it's just the whole population split  up and put in order from the lightest to  the heaviest of the lightest mass to the  greatest mass and every single  percentile is a hundredth of the full  number thank you
 

